{"ratings":[{"id":"C1","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Log lines that resemble valid unnumbered TAP failures can fill the excerpt limit and hide the actual assertion."},{"id":"C2","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A sufficiently long failure block can cause implementer feedback to omit the assertion and test name."},{"id":"C3","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Bun's failure recap can duplicate excerpts and produce a misleading omission notice."},{"id":"C4","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"When excerpts exist, the land log and artifact view hide earlier portions of the stored output tail."},{"id":"C5","consequence":"secret","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Failure excerpts can persist credentials that the previous output tail would have discarded, and clipping can defeat later literal redaction."},{"id":"C6","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"An evaluation case containing a failed gate result with the new field is rejected instead of loading."},{"id":"C7","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Near the excerpt size limit, land formatting can truncate diagnostic text or damage an omission notice."}]}